// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row wrapper_unsize_missing_impl_backend_diag (tier 4, `diag`).
// The VERDICT is right and the SENTENCE is not sema's: an ill-typed single-field
// wrapper unsize — `Rc<A>` to `Rc<dyn Other>` where `A` does NOT implement
// `Other` — passes the sema coercion pipeline and is caught by the BACKEND:
//     mlir_gen: internal: no vtable for 'A' as '&dyn Other' — the fat pointer's
//     vtable half would be left uninitialised and the first dispatch would jump
//     through stack garbage
//     mlir_gen: 1 self-diagnosed malfunction(s) — COMPILE FAILED
// rustc says E0277 ("the trait bound `A: Other` is not satisfied") at the
// coercion, and the object file is not written either way — so this is a
// SENTENCE defect, not an admission. `try_struct_unsize_coerce` tests the
// SHAPE of the unsize (one field, sized -> DstRef/TraitObject/Slice) and never
// asks whether the trait is implemented; the analogous check exists for
// AGGREGATE elements ("aggregate element {}: slot type {} needs an unsize
// from {}, but the type does not implement the trait…", sema_expr.cpp) and is
// not reached from this path.
//
// MEASURED 2026-09-08 on the BASE binary 2c5a33ef99e94fc9 43 at the `let` door
// exactly as written below — i.e. THIS ROW PREDATES the method-selector repair
// landed in the same commit; that repair only routes the METHOD-ARGUMENT door
// into the same pre-existing hole, and the two doors print the identical
// sentence. Re-measured on 69440e4620b32dcb 43: unchanged.
package wrapper_unsize_missing_impl_backend_diag;
use logos.lang.rc;

trait Sp { fn v(&self) -> i64; }
trait Other { fn w(&self) -> i64; }
struct A { x: i64 }
impl Sp for A { fn v(&self) -> i64 { return self.x; } }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let rc: Rc<A> = rc_new(A { x: 17i64 });
    let d: Rc<dyn Other> = rc;
    if d.w() != 17i64 { return 10i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
